  "text": "We are given the cubic equation:\n\n$$\nz^3 + az^2 + bz + c = 0\n$$\n\nLet the roots be $ r_1, r_2, r_3 $.\n\nWe are to determine the **sum of the squares of the imaginary parts** of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\left| \\text{Im}(r_1) \\right|^2 + \\left| \\text{Im}(r_2) \\right|^2 + \\left| \\text{Im}(r_3) \\right|^2\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use Vieta's formulas\n\nFor a cubic equation of the form:\n\n$$\nz^3 + az^2 + bz + c = 0\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $ r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -a $\n- $ r_1r_2 + r_1r_3 + r_2r_3 = b $\n- $ r_1r_2r_3 = -c $\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the identity for the sum of squares of imaginary parts\n\nWe recall the identity:\n\n$$\n| \\text{Im}(r_1) |^2 + | \\text{Im}(r_2) |^2 + | \\text{Im}(r_3) |^2 = \\text{Re}( (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 ) - \\text{Re}( (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 )\n$$\n\nWait — that’s not quite right. Let’s think differently.\n\nWe can write:\n\n$$\n| \\text{Im}(r_1) |^2 + | \\text{Im}(r_2) |^2 + | \\text{Im}(r_3) |^2\n$$\n\nLet’s denote $ \\text{Im}(r_i) = y_i $, so we need:\n\n$$\n|y_1|^2 + |y_2|^2 + |y_3|^2\n$$\n\nNow, recall that:\n\n$$\n|y|^2 = (y \\cdot \\overline{y}) = \\text{Re}(y \\cdot \\overline{y}) = \\text{Re}( (y_1 + y_2 + y_3)^2 )\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n|y_1|^2 + |y_2|^2 + |y_3|^2 = \\text{Re}( (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 )\n$$\n\nSo we need to compute:\n\n$$\n\\text{Re}( (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 )\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Compute $ (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 $\n\nWe have:\n\n$$\nr_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -a \\Rightarrow (r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 = (-a)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n|y_1|^2 + |y_2|^2 + |y_3|^2 = \\text{Re}(a^2)\n$$\n\nNow, since $ a $ is a complex number, $ \\text{Re}(a^2) $ is just the real part of $ a^2 $, which is a real number.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{ \\text{Re}(a^2) }\n$$\n\nThis is the sum of the squares of the imaginary parts of the roots.",
